# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
#
# RDR-9: the map comes back after the reader's full screen, and the key
# that opens it was not spent while it was away (#332).
#
# **Why this leg exists.** With both seams on: Tab, `Notes`, `EXIT`. The
# reader's full screen is given back by asking the program to compose the
# adventuring screen, the composer repaints the party roster, and the
# automap's panel is drawn *into* the roster's cells — so composing is
# exactly the thing that erases the map. Nothing told the automap, so its
# pixels were gone and its bookkeeping said they were there: the player's
# next Tab was spent *closing* a panel that was not on the screen, and
# only the one after it brought the map back. Seen on a display, and then
# driven.
#
# The cause is in `seam.h` rather than in either seam. **While a batch of
# calls into the program is running the engine offers no points at all**,
# so the automap's own clear-region and roster-drawn points — the two it
# watches its cells with — cannot see a single pixel of a give-back made
# through the program's own routines. The give-back says so itself now
# (`automap_state::note_panel_painted_over()`), and the map's next
# arrival draws it again over the screen the program has just composed:
# the program paints first and the seam paints after, which is the
# ordering #303 settled for the Encamp Fix's report.
#
# **A single leg, and it has to be one**, for `not-log-giveback.leg`'s
# reason and one more of its own. `N` reaches the program in a seam-off
# run, where the adventuring bar answers an unknown letter by stepping
# its own highlight, so the two runs differ on the bar row from that
# moment whatever the give-back does (#234). And Tab is the automap's,
# so a seam-off run has no map to compare against at all.
#
# So the claim is stated directly and more strongly: **the screen with
# the map up before the reader opened is the screen with the map up
# after it closed**, every pixel but the bar row — and the screen after
# the two Tabs that follow is that screen again, which is what says the
# first of them closed a panel that was really there rather than being
# swallowed by one that was not.
#
# **Driven, on the shorter boot, by hand.** This script is the boot #291
# left — the code-wheel seam on and told the challenge is answered — and
# the runner does not yet carry that flag (#293 owes every leg its
# re-drive on the new boot), so this leg was driven by hand with the same
# host, the same disk copy, the same dump cadence and the same checks the
# runner makes — its own `check_single`, over the stills this script
# dumped: *121 frames held still, 2 given back*, nothing wrong. Both
# `equal` lines pass at 181 pixels differing, every one of them on the
# bar row. Before the change, the same script, the same disk and the
# same frames leave the roster where the map was, and the runner says
# so: `frame 10600 is not frame 9000 again: 1633 pixels differ, box
# 136,16,311,62`. Those 1,633 pixels are still there at 11,400, after
# the first Tab — the whole of the defect, in one number — and only at
# 12,200, after the second, is the map back.
about the automap comes back after the journal's full screen, and Tab still works
kind single
program START.EXE
both code-wheel
seam automap
seam journal
until 280000000
every 25

# Slot C, the city, and the party's own command bar. Then Tab for the
# map, `Notes` for the log — the empty log, which takes the whole screen
# just as a full one does (`not-log-giveback.leg`) — and `E` for the way
# out. Then Tab twice: the first must take the map away and the second
# must bring it back, which is the half of this a stale open flag gets
# wrong.
key L@7550
key C@7800
key Tab@8900
key N@9600
key E@10400
key Tab@11000
key Tab@11600

# The bar row, and it is excepted rather than owned: the give-back injects
# a space to make the menu-bar routine return and draw itself again, and
# the routine draws itself with the highlight at its first command
# (#234). What comes back is the right bar with the right words.
rect bar 0,190,319,199

# The map, up and holding still with nothing pressed.
same 9000-9575
# The log, settled over it. It is slow to appear — painted a few rows an
# arrival by the program's own drawing routines, about 130 frames for a
# listing (#305) — so this starts after the paint rather than in it.
same 9775-10375
# The map back, and holding still again.
same 10600-10975
# And back once more after the two Tabs.
same 11625-13000

# The whole of #332, as two frames. The map is up in both, the roster is
# under it in both, the viewport, the status line and the ornaments are
# the program's own in both.
equal 9000 10600 bar
# And the key was not spent: Tab took the map away and Tab brought it
# back, so the screen after them is the screen before them.
equal 9000 12200 bar
